huttar@hp750.itg.ti.com (Lars Huttar) writes:

>An interesting idea...
>It wouldn't be just a matter of computing the distance, though:
>you'd actually have to "run" the creature through N time units,
>allowing it to choose its directions, run into traps, use its
>wands if it so desired, etc.... and then you might run into inconsistencies
>because of the fact that one monster is "living" while any others on
>the levels are "frozen".  So you'd have to run the whole level simultaneously.
>And then what if you had chased two or more monsters down the stairs at
>different times before going down... etc.


That's a good point.  One solution is to re-animate the whole level
when you enter it, running the level as a whole, including all
creatures on it, through N time units.  This could take a lot of time
if you return to a level after 10,000 moves, but perhaps an *exact
simulation* of level life wouldn't be necessary, with all the individual
movements of monsters stepped through, etc., just a pared-down routine
that checks important stuff like scared monsters, rotting corpses,
monster healing, and other timeouts, and maybe shifting the positions
of monsters around once according to a quick formula.
In fact, much of this may take place already, I haven't been too
observant.  It would be nice if visited levels undergo some sort
of simulated life while the player is away elsewhere.

-jim


